# [% site_name %]

> [% site_name %] documentation and site pages.

Each link below is the page's Markdown source rather than its rendered HTML -
the same words without the markup, and the same access rules (SM825). A page
that needs a sign-in to read answers these the way it answers any other
request.
[%- # SM1357: the organisation entry, from the site's own details in
    # lazysite.conf (site_type, site_address, site_phone, site_email, and
    # site_hours naming a schedule). Absent on a site that states none. %]
[% IF organisation %]
## About

- Name: [% organisation.name %]
[% IF organisation.type != 'Organization' %]- Kind: [% organisation.type %]
[% END %][% IF organisation.url %]- Website: [% organisation.url %]
[% END %][% IF organisation.address %]- Address: [% organisation.address %]
[% END %][% IF organisation.telephone %]- Telephone: [% organisation.telephone %]
[% END %][% IF organisation.email %]- Email: [% organisation.email %]
[% END %][% IF organisation.hours.size %]
### Opening hours

[% FOREACH line IN organisation.hours %]- [% line %]
[% END %][% END %][% END %]
## Pages

[% FOREACH page IN pages %]
[%- # SM299: the source file's URL, which is NOT always the page URL plus ".md".
    # An index page's URL already ends in a slash, so appending ".md" produced
    # "<dir>/.md" - not a file. The homepage is an index page, so this was the
    # FIRST entry of every site's llms.txt, and the one an AI client is most
    # likely to follow.
    #
    # page.path looks like an easier answer and is wrong: it is docroot-relative,
    # so on a content-rooted domain it carries the sites/<name>/ prefix, which is
    # part of no public URL.
    src = page.url.search('/$') ? page.url _ 'index.md' : page.url _ '.md' -%]
- [[% page.title %]]([% site_url %][% src %])[% IF page.meta_desc %]: [% page.meta_desc %][% END %]
[% END %]
